      "children": [
        "card-0001",
        "board-0001"
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": {
          "value": 100,
          "unit": "%"
        },
        "height": {
          "value": 100,
          "unit": "%"
        },
        "flexDirection": "column",
        "alignItems": "center",
        "justifyContent": "center",
        "backgroundColor": "var(--background)",
        "paddingTop": "var(--space-20)"
      },
      "metadata": {
        "comment": "Ungraded furniture, already in the starter. justifyContent:center does not resolve here because the Page root gives no definite height, so the top spacing is padding rather than centring — deliberate, and the reason height:100% is left in place is that it costs nothing and is what a learner would write."
      },
      "x": 100,
      "y": 160
    },
    {
      "id": "card-0001",
      "type": "Group",
      "label": "Card",
      "parent": "shell-0001",
      "children": [
        "creature-0001",
        "name-0001"
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "sizeMode": "contentSize",
        "flexDirection": "column",
        "alignItems": "center",
        "rowGap": "var(--space-4)",
        "backgroundColor": "var(--surface)",
        "borderRadius": "var(--radius-xl)",
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderWidth": "var(--border-1)",
        "borderColor": "var(--border)",
        "paddingLeft": "var(--space-8)",
        "paddingRight": "var(--space-8)",
        "paddingTop": "var(--space-8)",
        "paddingBottom": "var(--space-8)"
      },
      "metadata": {
        "comment": "The coloured frame. sizeMode contentSize is graded on purpose: a Group is 100% x 100% by default, and a card that swallows the screen is the first wall a beginner hits."
      },
      "x": 160,
      "y": 280
    },
    {
      "id": "creature-0001",
      "type": "Circle",
      "label": "Creature",
      "parent": "card-0001",
      "parameters": {
        "size": 96,
        "fillColor": "var(--primary)"
      },
      "metadata": {
        "comment": "The creature itself. One Circle, deliberately: nothing ships an image, and eyes would need absolute positioning, which is lesson 2's subject."
      },
      "x": 220,
      "y": 400
    },
    {
      "id": "name-0001",
      "type": "Text",
      "label": "Name",
      "parent": "card-0001",
      "parameters": {
        "text": "Nibbles",
        "fontSize": "var(--text-xl)",
        "fontWeight": "var(--font-semibold)",
        "color": "var(--foreground)"
      },
      "x": 220,
      "y": 520
    },
    {
      "id": "board-0001",
      "type": "Group",
      "label": "Board",
      "x": 160,
      "y": 640,
      "parent": "shell-0001",
      "parameters": {
        "sizeMode": "contentHeight",
        "maxWidth": {
          "value": 560,
          "unit": "px"
        },
        "marginTop": "var(--space-6)",
        "paddingLeft": "var(--space-4)",
        "paddingRight": "var(--space-4)"
      },
      "metadata": {
        "comment": "Step 2's node. sizeMode contentHeight + maxWidth is the responsive sizing idea in one node: take the width you are given, stop at 560, be as tall as your contents. The horizontal padding is NOT decoration — a Columns node draws its gutters with negative margins, so at full width its container is marginX wider than its parent and gets clipped. Padding is what gives the gutter somewhere to go. Graded with hasParams rather than paramsEqual because the amount genuinely does not matter."
      },
      "children": [
        "care-0001"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "care-0001",
      "type": "net.noodl.visual.columns",
      "label": "Care",
      "x": 220,
      "y": 760,
      "parent": "board-0001",
      "parameters": {
        "layoutString": "1 1 1",
        "smallBreakpoint": {
          "value": 480,
          "unit": "px"
        },
        "smallLayout": "1"
      },
      "metadata": {
        "comment": "Steps 3 and 5. 🔴 smallBreakpoint measures THIS NODE'S CONTAINER, not the viewport (Columns.tsx:205, NDA-006 §3). Inside a 560-wide Board with 32px of padding the strip is 528 on a laptop and 358 on a phone, so 480 is the number that separates them. A viewport-shaped number like 600 folds the columns at every width, which is what the first draft of this lesson did."
      },
      "children": [
        "feed-0001",
        "play-0001",
        "sleep-0001"
      ]
    },
    {
